.th SPEED I Feb/1976
.sh NAME
speed \*- do lineprinter escapes and overstrikes
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd speed
[ file ... ]
.sh DESCRIPTION
The output of
.it speed
is the concatenation of the
files whose names are its arguments
with the characters {, }, |, ~, and ` replaced by the corresponding
single case lineprinter escapes.
As well, all backspaces are removed, and overstrikes are done by inserting
CR characters and overprinting two or more lines.
Unlike the lineprinter driver, which does roughly the same thing,
speed guarantees to print singly overstruck lines in at most two
lines of output.
If no input files are mentioned, the standard input is used.
Thus,
.it speed
may be used as a filter.
For text with a lot of escapes, or a large number of underscored words,
this can amount to considerable time saving over letting the lineprinter
driver do the work.
.sh "SEE ALSO"
lp(IV), lpr(I)
.sh DIAGNOSTICS
A message is printed on the diagnostic output stream for each file that
could not be opened.
.sh AUTHOR
Tom Duff
.sh BUGS
Does not understand any control characters other than NL, BS, CR, and HT.
Thus, in particular, formfeeds can cause peculiar results.
